<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lord Xinling</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 243 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Wei Wuji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lord Xinling, personal name Wei Wuji, was a prince of the State of Wei and one of the Four Lords of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, famed for their thousands of retainers. In 257 BCE he stole his brother's military tally, seized command of the Wei army and relieved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handan">Handan</a>. In 247 BCE he led an alliance of five states that defeated Qin, but he died out of favour in 243 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lord-xinling.jpg" alt="Lord Xinling" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 清蔡奡評點 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NJlib-0026095-0001_%E6%9D%B1%E5%91%A8%E5%88%97%E5%9C%8B%E5%BF%97%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%81%E4%B8%83%E5%8D%B7%E4%B8%80%E7%99%BE%E5%85%AB%E5%9B%9E%E5%9C%96%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%B7_(%E4%BF%A1%E9%99%B5%E5%90%9B).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
